ity issues.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f607dd457c67f9209","impact":0.4,"is_primary":null,"location":"","norm_id":"ned06ebc801a522e3","priority":0.4,"reason":null,"recalled":false,"scenarios":[],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"Split compound criteria, replace subjective terms with observable outcomes, and state how each is verified.","tier":"DET","validity":1.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State whether the retry-budget and fuse thresholds are centralized/configurable (single policy source) vs. hardcoded, so they can evolve without multi-component edits.","cohort":["T12","T2","T5e","T7"],"criteria":["T5e"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'At most three physical invocations and 15 seconds cumulative sleep per logical postcondition'","'opens after three consecutive exhausted eligible logical outcomes across at least two tickets. Provider scope additionally requires two endpoint families.'","ABSENCE: no statement of where these thresholds live (config vs. constant) or how they change"],"finding":"The approved-policy thresholds (three physical invocations, 15s cumulative sleep, ~2s/4s fallback delays, three-consecutive-failure fuse, two-endpoint-family provider scope) are stated as fixed policy but the plan does not say whether they are configurable or hardcoded constants; these are the kind of tuning values likely to evolve as real Cloud/DC rate behavior is observed.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f1bd747ee4dd51dcc","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approved policy section","norm_id":"nbb43cb4160597acb","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Operators discover the 15s budget or 3-consecutive fuse threshold needs tuning against real provider rate limits after rollout"],"severity":"none","suggested_fix":"Add a note that all budget/fuse thresholds live in one policy value/config consumed by